    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so basically because the angiotensin II is low, the vessels stay more dilated and everything is more relaxed. can you help me visualize the actual binding process at the enzyme site? like how does the drug physically interfere with the enzyme's ability to process the substrate?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril acts as a competitive inhibitor that structurally resembles the catalytic substrate (Angiotensin I) but lacks the necessary chemical groups for the enzyme's active site to perform chloropeptidic cleavage. By occupying the binding pocket of the enzyme, it physically prevents Angiotensin I from docking and sliding through the hinge region required to facilitate the hydrolytic reaction. Consequently, the enzyme cannot complete its catalytic cycle, effectively stalling the production of Angiotensin II at the molecular level.",
      "source": "target",
